# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone** — no equivalent needed since abort can't happen
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be thrown** — dead code path

## Modern .NET 8 Implementation

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't stop within timeout
            // No force-kill equivalent — design GetNextItemAsync to respect the token
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()` ignores cancellation** | If the original blocks on a `Queue.Dequeue` or DB call, it won't see `_running = false`. Use `Channel<T>`, `BlockingCollection.Take(token)`, or async I/O that accepts a `CancellationToken`. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | It didn't reliably unblock waiting threads before either. `CancellationToken` propagates into async waits. |
| **No forced termination exists** | You **must** design cooperative cancellation into every blocking call. There is no `Abort()` safety net. |
| **`Task.Run` vs `Thread`** | Uses the thread pool — appropriate for long-running I/O. If CPU-bound, use `Task.Factory.StartNew(..., TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning)`. |
| **Exception semantics differ** | `OperationCanceledException` is *not* automatically rethrown like `ThreadAbortException` was — no `ResetAbort()` needed. |

## If `GetNextItem()` Can't Be Made Async

For legacy sync code that truly blocks without token support:

```csharp
// Wrap in a dedicated thread, but still use CancellationToken for the outer loop
_workerTask = Task.Factory.StartNew(() =>
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        var item = GetNextItem(); // sync block — ensure it has its own timeout
        if (token.IsCancellationRequested) break;
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
}, token, TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning, TaskScheduler.Default);
```

Add an internal timeout or sentinel value to `GetNextItem()` so the loop can re-check cancellation periodically.

## Summary

The core design shift: **.NET 8 requires cooperative cancellation** — you can no longer forcibly kill a thread. Pass `CancellationToken` through every blocking operation, and use `OperationCanceledException` as the structured replacement for `ThreadAbortException`.
